<title id="_15e14002-ac5e-3b9c-c3e0-b87a27a6134c">The series</title>
<p id="_822f4480-4722-4c4c-8fb6-971f8c54899b">The tebako ecosystem packages applications and runtimes into single executables whose loading behavior is governed by normative specifications: the wire format of the package container, the manifest grammar of a payload, the contract between the loader and a runtime, and the policies for resolution, caching, jailing, trust, and observability. Those normative texts live as Markdown files in the product repository, and an implementer — a runtime factory author, a registry operator, a feedstock maintainer — has until now read them only as raw Markdown source, with no rendered document to cite.</p>

<p id="_627552a9-bb36-524e-0417-66eed5cca76e">The Tebako Specifications (TBK SPEC) series closes that gap. Each document in the series is one normative text from the repository, converted to a Metanorma standard document and rendered to HTML and PDF as a member of a single collection. The document numbering follows the numbering of the source files: TBK SPEC 02 renders  <tt>02-tpkg-wire-format.md</tt>, and so on. Section numbering, section titles, tables, and code listings are carried over verbatim, so a reference of the form "spec 06 §4" resolves identically against the Markdown source and against the rendered document.</p>
</clause>

<clause id="_c4a6cfed-af9e-3001-f605-bbb3f8838d75" obligation="normative">
<title id="_e3ca4492-cec4-8103-e24f-623d15e6fed6">Source of truth and synchronization</title>
<p id="_89b1b07b-d135-ed40-3c90-0c0c490d5fab">The Markdown files under <tt>docs/spec/</tt> in the <tt>tamatebako/tebako</tt> repository are the source of truth for every document in the series. This series is their rendered form; the AsciiDoc twin of a specification is derived from the Markdown and never edited independently. The Markdown sources themselves are not modified by the rendering process.</p>

<p id="_93874d75-4d76-3690-be4c-d56aeb723510">Each rendered document records the blob SHA of the Markdown file it was converted from in a comment line of the form <tt>// source: docs/spec/NN-name.md @ &lt;sha&gt;</tt>. The drift gate at <tt>docs/spec-site/tools/sync.rb</tt> recomputes the blob SHA of every referenced Markdown source and compares it with the recorded value. The gate exits with status 0 when every rendered twin is current, and exits 1 naming the drifted documents when a Markdown source has moved without its rendered twin being updated. A continuous-integration job runs the gate on every pull request that touches  <tt>docs/spec/</tt>, so a specification edit cannot land without its rendered twin following it.</p>

<p id="_45f35638-ba0d-cdb7-668c-53f2532b1976">The conversion from Markdown to AsciiDoc is performed and reviewed by hand; the gate does not regenerate document content. After a specification edit has been carried into its twin and reviewed, <tt>ruby docs/spec-site/tools/sync.rb --sync</tt> updates the recorded SHAs to bless the new state.</p>
</clause>

